Nothing is sent to TALOS — there is no TALOS to send it to. What does leave, leaves for the service you chose:

- your messages, to the cloud provider that answers, when it is a cloud model;
- a search’s words, to the search service you set;
- a page’s address, when TALOS reads that page;
- a model download, from Hugging Face.

Each of these is an off-device action, and passes the off-device power. A model on the device sends nothing at all.

A local model is a GGUF file run through llama.cpp, inside the app. What you write to it never leaves the device.

Before anything is downloaded, TALOS measures this device — free memory, storage, temperature and memory speed — and checks every variant against it:

- Memory: room to spare — it fits, with RAM left once it is loaded;
- Memory: little room — it fits, but under load Android may close TALOS;
- Speed: reads from disk — it barely fits, and the device would re-read the weights for every word;
- Not enough memory — it would not run.

The verdict states the context length it was checked at, the RAM left after loading, and a predicted speed. A ledger shows where every byte comes from — weights, KV cache, compute, runtime, and the safety margin — and which numbers are measured, predicted or fixed. Memory is measured against what the model really keeps resident, not against the peak a memory-mapped file appears to use.

The graphics processor is used only after this device passes a real test, never because of a chipset’s name. See Run a model on the device.

One limit: a model on the device cannot drive a Code session yet. Choosing one there is refused with that reason; cloud providers work.

Three consents in Settings → Privacy and permissions, under Content and models, decide what reaches a model at all:

- Library access — whether TALOS can search and read your Library files;
- Writing memories — whether it can save memories for future conversations;
- Sending attached images — whether an image you attach can be sent to the service that answers.

When a tool asks, a card in the chat shows what it is about to do, with its input:

- Allow runs this call and saves nothing;
- Always allow saves a grant for this tool on this device — the card itself carries the “always”, so asking costs one question per tool;
- Don’t allow stops it, and the model is told you declined;
- Decide later leaves the card for later.

Several steps asked at once can arrive as one plan, from which you take steps out before approving the rest. See Consent.

Under Approval duration:

- This message — the approval ends with the answer;
- The conversation, while trusted — it lasts for the whole conversation, until an untrusted page or document enters it. Then it expires, and the next use asks again.

Once TALOS is the assistant, calling it from any app opens a bar over that app instead of taking you away:

- it can see the screen you are on, and says how much of it — “I can see your screen”, with the number of elements;
- you write or speak, and attach photos, a camera shot, a file, or something from your Library;
- you can switch the model;
- Open in TALOS continues the conversation in the app.

Open a model. Its page has three tabs: Quantizations, Model card and Files. Every quantization is checked against this device as soon as the page opens:

- Memory: room to spare, Memory: little room, Speed: reads from disk or Not enough memory;
- the context length the check assumed — a smaller one needs less memory;
- a predicted speed, in tokens per second.

Where every byte comes from breaks the estimate down — weights, KV cache, compute, runtime and the safety margin — and Configure the runtime sets the context length and the KV cache type (Automatic, F16 or Q8_0).

TALOS also warns before you download: a file Hugging Face has flagged, a repository missing some of its parts, a file with no published checksum, or a model that does not list your language.

- Open the task and tap Edit, then turn on Let it repeat on its own.
- Under What it should do, write the instruction — for example, “summarize today’s AI news”. A repeating task must say what to do.
- Choose How often:
- Once, on a date and at a time;
- Every day, at a time;
- On chosen days, at a time;
- At intervals, every so many minutes — at least 15: Android will not wake an app more often than that.
- Turn on Only tell me if something changed to stay quiet when a run gives the same result as the last one. It still runs.
- Save. The task shows its next run; a schedule that would never run says so.

A scheduled task appears under Scheduled. Pause stops it until you Resume it.

A search sends its words off the device, so it needs the off-device power, in Settings → Agent Tools:

- on Ask me every time, the first search asks, and you can grant it once or for good;
- on Always allow, searches run without asking;
- on Never allow, no search runs — even with a key saved.

Search and page reading also save their sources in the Library, so they need Create or change things as well.

- A conversation is compacted the way TALOS Desktop compacts it. The CLI now runs the Desktop’s own kernel module for each turn instead of the bare kernel. Without the Context Engine (an unknown window, a window under 64,000 tokens, a local model) a long conversation was summarized every 8 rounds, the summary kept only the first two messages (often not the task), the summarizer was offered the tools, and every tool result was cut to 8,000 characters. Now:
- the system messages at the start, your first request, the last three requests and the last two exchanges are kept word for word, and everything between them is summarized; a first request longer than 8,000 characters keeps its first 6,000 and last 2,000, with a note of what was left out; a request with an image counts as a request;
- it starts at 0.75 of the model’s window, or at context.compactAtTokens when that is lower (200,000 by default);
- the summarizer gets no tools;
- a summary is asked only when it can make the conversation smaller. When what is kept word for word already fills the space (a long paste in your latest requests, with little before it), only the long tool results among them are shortened, if there are any, and no summary is paid for nothing; a summary that comes back longer than what it replaces is not used;
- after three failed summaries in the same turn a mechanical one is used;
- tool results are no longer cut at 8,000 characters: they reach the model whole, and are shortened only when the conversation would not fit, with a note of how many characters were left out. With the Context Engine, a failed preparation falls back to that compaction instead of failing the turn, and the screen says so in one line with its code.
- The window of the model is known to the compaction. The CLI’s own model profile (your models setting, a local Ollama’s window, the provider’s list, models.dev) now gives the compaction its window and output limit. With an unknown window the conversation is compacted at 200,000 tokens.
- Summaries are written by the provider’s cheapest model. A compaction’s summary goes to the light model of the same provider (DeepSeek Pro’s to DeepSeek Flash), measured on the smaller of the two windows. When the light model’s window is unknown, or too small for the summary, the conversation’s model writes it. The conversation keeps the model you chose.
