The approval card offers, by number:

- Allow once;
- Allow for this session;
- Always allow — an allow rule is written for that operation;
- Deny, and the turn goes on;
- Always deny — a deny rule is written (in the full review, opened with v).

Esc denies and stops the turn.

- Open a terminal in a project and run talos.
- The first time, a short setup asks only what is missing, one step at a time:
- whether to trust the folder;
- a provider — a local engine that is running comes first;
- its key, typed in hidden input;
- a model.
- Esc skips a step. /setup in the interactive screen, or talos setup from the shell, runs it again.

A key already in your environment, such as OPENAI_API_KEY, is used only after you agree once. A key you type is kept in the operating system’s credential store, never in a file.

/undo — also /rewind or /checkpoint — opens Go back to an earlier point: the checkpoints of this conversation, each with the files that changed since it and would go back. Pick a point, then choose what goes back:

- Enter — the code and the conversation;
- c — the conversation only;
- f — the files only.

Going back first saves a checkpoint of the present, so you can return to it. When a turn ends, its last line names its checkpoint: Checkpoint · 1 file: src/retry.mjs (modified) · /undo goes back.

