For larger work the agent can propose a workflow: a draft in phases and steps that the server checks before you see it. With Coordination off, proposing starts nothing: you review it, approve it and start it. With Coordination on, an approved workflow starts by itself, and its outcome wakes the conversation.

You follow its run step by step, and pause, resume or cancel it. A step that failed or ended uncertain can be:

- Mark as done… — you write what was done instead; the steps that wait for it read that as its result;
- Set aside… — it is not redone; the steps that wait for it do not start, the others go on;
- Redo with another model… — it starts again from scratch, in a new session, on the model you choose;
- Resume, checking first… — it continues in its own session, first checking whether the attempt before already did the work.

At the run’s ceiling, Raise the ceiling and resume continues it. The operations behind it are in Workflows.

The model of a session is changed from the bar at the top of the chat. Settings → Model Lab has four tabs:

- Hugging Face — models that run on this computer: those to download, and those installed;
- Provider — the providers, their keys, and the catalog of their models;
- Downloads — every download, with its progress, pauses and recovery;
- System — this computer, telling what is measured, estimated or still unknown.

For each model of the catalog you see its provider, how much context it holds, what it takes in and gives out (text, images, audio, files), whether it can use tools, and its prices — a price the provider does not declare reads “not declared”, never zero.

A model in the catalog is a model that exists, not one your access already covers.

When TALOS asks before an action and you answer No, nothing is done, and that is not a failure:

- the step reads not allowed, in a neutral colour, never green and never red;
- the card closes with You said no, in grey, also when you answered from another window; on the card of an automation or of the providers it reads Cancelled, in grey too. The Deny button is grey as well: saying no is the safe choice;
- the summary of the turn counts it apart: “1 step not allowed”, not with what worked or what failed;
- the model is told that you said no, and not to ask again for the same thing, by a card or by a question, unless you bring it up. It goes on without it, or says in one line what it can do instead.

A refusal that comes from a rule — a blocked folder, a tool on Deny — is not your no: it still reads as refused.

The list of sessions: each row is a piece of work, with its title, its model and its state. States are ordered by what needs you first:

- waiting for you — the agent stopped on a question;
- running;
- waiting for the first message — created, never started;
- interrupted — closed half way, or no longer run by anyone;
- stopped — you stopped it. Stopping is neither finishing nor failing;
- done;
- error — or, when the reason is known, the reason itself, such as “turns exhausted”;
- done · outcome not recorded — an old session with no saved outcome. It does not mean it succeeded.

Turns and model show only when the server counted or declared them. Click a row to open it, right-click it for its actions, or select several. A session can be resumed after you close and reopen TALOS.

### Pin a session

Hover a row and press the star, or choose Pin to top in its menu: the session moves under Pinned, at the top of the list. Unpin puts it back. The pin is kept by TALOS with the session itself, not by one window: a phone connected to this computer sees the same pins when it reads the list again, and deleting a session takes its pin away. Pins you made before this version move to TALOS once, by themselves. If a pin cannot be saved, TALOS says “The star could not be saved: try again.”

What TALOS must remember beyond one conversation. Each memory has a kind:

- Preference — how you want things done;
- Fact — something true about the project;
- Procedure — how a thing is done;
- Rule — a constraint to respect.

Memory is not a recording of the conversation: a memory exists because the agent wrote it, when it judged it useful, or because you did. A new session starts with your memories in its prompt, up to a cap, and the model finds the rest with its memory search — see Memory, and what stays out. That is why reading memory counts as a higher risk than reading a note in the Tool Forge: a memory written today is read again in future conversations.

- In the composer, press + and choose where it comes from:
- a file of the project — from this session’s folder;
- a file from the disk — any file of your computer;
- an image — a photo or a diagram to look at;
- your last screenshot.
- Look at the cost shown next to each attachment: an estimate, in tokens.
- Send.

An image must finish uploading before you send. If you send too early, TALOS tells you the image was not uploaded and asks you to attach it again, instead of sending an empty reference.

Two other ways keep a document within reach without paying for it at every message:

- a file of instructions in the project, which the model reads with every message — see Project instructions;
- the Library, which the model opens only when it needs a file.

- access to a provider;
- the environment commands run in;
- version control;
- the browsing tool;
- the agent’s service;
- the catalog of predefined tasks;
- the project folders allowed;
- the configuration of web search;
- providers and the keyring;
- experimental features that are on;
- the restore of sessions, with which ones were dropped and why.

When the model reads a .docx, .xlsx, .pptx or .pdf file, it gets its text — not a note saying the file is binary. The limits are the same as Hermes Agent’s:

- documents up to 50 MB;
- spreadsheets: only the visible sheets, each under its own name, up to 5,000 rows and 256 columns per sheet;
- a PDF protected by a password is refused, saying so;
- when many pages of a PDF give no text — scanned pages, or blank ones — the model is told first, with the list of the missing pages, instead of reading an answer into the gaps;
- a document with no text at all is an error the model sees, never an empty answer.

The extracted text is then read a part at a time, like any other file.

- Open Settings → Agent tools and permissions, then the source of web search.
- Choose one:
- DuckDuckGo, no key — the default, which works without setting anything up;
- Tavily or Brave Search — with a key;
- SearXNG — your own instance, by its address;
- A custom endpoint — any API that answers with a list of results at its top level; the key is optional;
- No web search — search switched off entirely. That is a choice, not a fault.
- If the source needs a key, enter it: it is saved in the operating system’s keyring, not in the window. An address is saved in a configuration file next to the server.
- Run a test search from the same panel.

While a source lacks its key or its address, the panel says which, and search stays off.

DuckDuckGo without a key is not an official service: TALOS reads its public results page, which may refuse under heavy use. When it does, the result says so instead of returning an empty list.

In the root of the session’s folder: .talos/hooks.json, or .harness-ui-hooks.json.

```
{
  "hooks": [
    { "id": "no-secrets", "eventi": ["pre_tool_call"], "comando": "node .talos/hooks/no-secrets.mjs" }
  ]
}
```

id names the hook (a name, not a path), eventi lists its events, comando is the command. The first file that declares an id wins.
